ed them 494 yards to 151 and held the ball 32:57 to 27:03.","Against the closing line (Draft Kings): Lions closed as 2.5-point favorites and covered by 22.5; the total closed at 50.5 and they combined for 45, under by 5.5."],"review":[{"title":"The algo's call","body":"Logged before the start, last snapshot Nov 15, 9:26 AM ET. It had Patriots 51% and Lions 49%, projecting Patriots 25.4 – Lions 25 (50.4 total, Patriots by 0.4).\n\nFinal: Patriots 10 – Lions 35. Wrong winner: it had Patriots at 51%, and Lions (49%) won."},{"title":"How the ratings held up","body":"Patriots came in with a 5% above average attack and a 5% stingier than average defense (5-4, 23.4 scored and 23 allowed a game over 9 games), worth about 42 and 45 games of evidence. Projected to score 25.4, they scored 10 (−15.4 points, 61% under); projected to allow 25, they allowed 35 (+10 points, 40% over).\n\nLions came in with a 14% above average attack and a league-average defense (6-2, 27.8 scored and 23.6 allowed a game over 8 games), worth about 42 and 43 games of evidence. Projected to score 25, they scored 35 (+10 points, 40% over); projected to allow 25.4, they allowed 10 (−15.4 points, 61% under).\n\nThe bigger miss was Patriots' scoring (-15.4 points against projection)."},{"title":"Where the result sat in the simulations","body":"Total: 45 against 50.4 projected (-5.4), about the 34th percentile of the simulated totals (a typical game swings ±13.2).\n\nMargin: Lions by 25 against Patriots by 0.4 projected, about the 98th percentile of the simulated margins (typical swing ±12.8).\n\nAn unusual result, but one the simulations saw regularly (roughly one game in six to one in twenty)."},{"title":"Situational factors, checked","body":"Nothing situational moved this projection (rest, travel, form and conditions were all normal), so it came down to team strength."},{"title":"Against the market","body":"DET ML (-148) at -148: the algo gave it 56% vs 57% fair (-0.8 pts) — won (+0.7u); lost to the clos